gerous the  atom bomb or the computer that was just  a couple years after the Hiroshima and  Nagasaki disasters but it's true if if  computers are replacing many kinds of  things that people are skilled to do  that's an issue we don't know where it's  going to go and that doesn't necessarily  have to work out that way but I think  it's a risk that we'll see greater  inequality in in 10 20 30 50 a hundred  years and we have to think I think we  should think about this one thing that  I've said in my in in my recent books we  should be thinking about this now not  wait until after it happens even though  we don't know that it's going to get  worse so I'm here's where I think  finance training helps me it's a risk  it's certainly a risk that this trend is  get much worse you'd hardly hear people  discussing that but what we're supposed  to be trained to do in finance is to  think about risks and how to manage them  and there are tools for risk management  so in particular I've advocated and  actually this was picked up by Ian Ayres  at the Yale Law School in a New York  Times column he wrote he and I are both  advocating that we have a contingency  plan in place now to raise taxes on the  rich  if inequality gets worse they won't like  it but I'll tell you they'll know  they'll accept it much more willingly if  it's done in advance as a contingency  plan then if we wait until it happens  and then try to be try to fix it yes  well I was getting out of the shower I  wasn't properly dressed but I didn't  want to miss the phone call so I took it  in our kitchen that's where I was and I  yeah and it was quite a quite a surprise  what had happened I I certainly didn't  expect it oh I called my brother yeah  well I thought my brother sleeps in and  he might actually not know in Detroit so  I called him and I said did you hear the  news and he says the Tigers lost  everyone else called me that's the only  person I called my brother yes  mm-hmm right well you you mentioned two 