 that's my list of  country now I don't necessarily know  that those are bubbles but I'm just  saying that people in those countries  say that so it's happening in so many  places it's hard to it's hard to  understand all of these phenomena what I  think is that after the financial crisis  or starting around 2000 before the  financial crisis the world became more  speculative about real estate it used to  be not considered something to worry  about home fire unless you lived in  Manhattan or a rare unusual place or  London face like that but now people  worry everywhere it's a cultural change  that I think will tend to bring  volatility for years to come  and I think it's a suitable function of  central bank's to try to lean against  this volatility which they're starting  to do and we have to we have to consider  that but it's it is it's a psychological  chain or a sociological change that on  the other hand let me just say about the  United States home prices are not high  yet here they've got a long way to go  before they're like they were in 2006 I  do with the support of the Yale School  of Management I do serve it we just did  a survey of home buyers and ask them  their expectations for home prices and  depending on how you quantify their or  summarize their expectations it seems  that Americans today who have just  bought a house thinking that  home market will be back up to its 2006  peak sometime in the 2030s or 2040 s I'm  reporting what they say homebuyers are  not excitedly optimistic about the  longer run they think in the short run  they're going up but they think that it  might be just a rebound and momenta say  also the advent of professional  investors like Blackstone Group which  now owns 36 thousand homes we're seeing  more professional involvement it  reflects a change in the market it's not  clear that it's the same thing that was  happening eight years ago when we saw  the housing market booming I'd say it's  probably not the same thing it has 